## Original Content
Mark Carney a affirmé, lundi, que les États-Unis avaient fait des demandes “inacceptables” qui “auraient mis en péril la culture et la langue française” au Canada. Le Premier ministre canadien en a fait une des raisons pour lesquelles il a claqué la porte des négociations avec Washington.

## Summary
Canada has defended its French language and sovereignty in a trade dispute with the United States. Mark Carney, identified as the Canadian Prime Minister, stated that US demands were unacceptable and would endanger French culture, leading him to walk out of negotiations with Washington.

## Key Points
- US demands in trade negotiations threatened French language and culture in Canada
- Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney rejected the demands and ended talks with the US
- The dispute is part of a broader commercial war between Canada and the United States
- The issue underscores the protection of cultural and linguistic heritage in international negotiations


